<section xml:id="section-isomorphisms">
  <title>Isomorphisms and the Major Theme</title>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-isomorphism">
    <title>Isomorphism</title>
    <p>
      In <xref ref="definition-homomorphism"/>, I defined a
      homonomorphism, which is a function that preserves group
      structure. Homomorphisms relate groups to each other and tie
      them together: a group can very much be understood by its
      homomorphisms to other groups. Now, in this section, I want to
      narrow to a special class of homomorphisms.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          An bijective homomorphism is called an
          <term>isomorphism</term>. Is there is an isomorphism between
          groups <m>G</m> and <m>H</m>, we will often write <m>H cong
          G</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      The greek roots of both of these word point at their use. The
      root <sq>morph</sq> means <sq>form</sq> or <sq>shape</sq>.
      Mathematicians use this root, in this context, to mean the
      structure of a group. Both of the roots <sq>homo</sq> and
      <sq>iso</sq> carry meaning of similarity or equality. In the
      mathematical useage, homomorphism means that the structure of
      the binary operation, specifically, is the same. In the result
      of <xref ref="section-homomorphism"/>, this also means that the
      identity and inverses were the same. This new word,
      isomorphism, is used more strongly. An isomorphism means that
      the entire group structure is the same, including the order of
      the group (the cardinal of the group as a set). 
    </p>
    <p>
      This is the big idea: two groups which have an isomophism
      between them are the same size and the group operation is
      preseved. This means that the two groups are, in some way,
      identity. If the groups are finite, then they will have
      equvalent Caley tables: just the names of the elements are
      changed. They will have the same subgroups with the same
      indices, the same conjugacy classes, and so on. This explains
      the notation <m>H \equiv G</m>: this is a kind of equality
      between groups.
    </p>
    <p>
      I'll say more in this in the next section, but now let me give
      some examples. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Consider the function <m>D_4 \rightarrow S_4</m> defined
        explicilty by this table. 
        <me>
          \begin{array}{c|cccccccc}
            g \amp \Id \amp R_1 \amp R_2 \amp R_3 
              \amp F_1 \amp F_2 \amp F_3 \amp F_4 \\
            \phi(g) \amp \Id \amp (1234) \amp (13)(24) \amp (1432) 
              \amp (24) \amp (12)(34) \amp (13) \amp (14)(23) 
          \end{array}
        </me>
        This explicit definition sends each symmetry of the square to
        the matching permutation of the vertices. For example,
        <m>F_2</m> switces vertices 1 and 2, and switches vertices 3
        and 4, so it is sent to the cycle which does the same, as a
        permutation: <m>(12)(34)</m>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        This is homomorphism. I'm not going to prove it, but you can
        check all the pair explicitly to verify it if you wish. It is
        not an isomorphism because <m>D_4</m> has eight element snad
        <m>S_4</m> has twenty-four; no bijecton can exist between
        these two sets. However, it is a bijection onto the subgroup
        <m>\langle (1234), (13) \rangle</m>. Again, I'm not going to
        prove this, but if you want, you can exhaustively verify that
        these two elements generate exactly the either elements which
        are the output of this function. Then <m>\phi</m> is a
        homomorhpism which is also a bijection, so it is an
        isomophisnm. I can write <m>D_4 \cong \langle (1234), (12)
        \rangle</m>, and I can say that <m>D_4</m> is isomorphic to a
        subgroup of <m>S_4</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        A similar isomorphism can be setup from <m>D_n</m> to an
        appropriate subgroup of <m>S_n</m> by looking at the elements
        of the dihedral group as permutation of the vertices. This
        also works for higher-dimensional symmetry groups. The cube in
        <m>\RR^3</m> has eight vertices, so the symmetries of the
        cube, by thinking about them as permutations of the vertices,
        are isomorphic to a subgroup of <m>S_8</m>. The dodecahedron
        has twenty vertices, so the symmetries of the dodecahedron are
        isomorphism to a subgroup of <m>S_{20}</m>. This just
        continues with more and more interesting symmetry groups in
        higher and higher dimensions. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Define a function <m>\phi: \ZZ/10/ZZ^* \rightarrow
        \ZZ/4\ZZ</m> by the discrete logarithm <m>\phi(n) = \ind_3
        n \mod 10</m>. (This is not the normal logarithm; the
        <sq>ind</sq> notation, instead of the normal <sq>log</sq>
        notation, it usual for these discrete logarithm. Explicity,
        the function is given by this table. 
        <me>
          \begin{array}{c|cccc}
            n \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 7 \amp 9 \\
            \phi(n) \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 2
          \end{array}
        </me>
        This is easy to verify: <m>3^2 = 9</m> and <m>3^3 = 7 \mod 10
        </m>. This is a logarithm, which means that the rules of
        logarithms do apply. In particular, <m>\ind_3 (mn) = \ind_3 m
        + \ind_3 n</m>. This is precisely the condition that make the
        map a homomorphisms. After this, I can see from the table that
        the function is bijective, so it is an isomorphism. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Consider this set of matrices.
        <me>
          G = \left\{ \begin{pmatrix} a \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1
          \end{pmatrix} \Bigg| a \in \RR^* \right\}.
        </me>
        This is a group under matrix multiplication, and a subgroup of
        <m>GL(n)</m>. Then consider the function <m>\phi: G
        \rightarrow \RR^*</m> defined by 
        <me>
          \phi \left( \begin{pmatrix} a \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1
          \end{pmatrix} \right) = a 
        </me>
        I claim that this function is an isomorphism. First, I need to
        check the homomorphism condition. Let <m>a,b \in \RR^*</m>. 
        <me>
          \phi \left( \begin{pmatrix} a \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1
          \end{pmatrix} 
          \begin{pmatrix} b \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1
          \end{pmatrix} \right) = 
          \phi \left( \begin{pmatrix} ab \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1
          \end{pmatrix} \right) = ab = 
          \phi \left( \begin{pmatrix} a \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1
          \end{pmatrix} \right) 
          \phi \left( \begin{pmatrix} b \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1
          \end{pmatrix} \right)
        </me>
        The steps here are just doing the matrix multiplication in the
        first step, applying the function, and then realizing that the
        two numbers are the outputs of the function for the two
        individual matrices. This is a homomorphism. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Then I need to prove this is a bijection. Surjectivity is
        pretty easy: for any non-zero real number <m>a \in \RR^*</m>,
        I can build the matrix with that entry in the upper left and
        then <m>\phi</m> sends this matrix to <m>a</m>, proving that
        all of <m>\RR^*</m> is in the output of the function.
      </p>
      <p>
        For injectivity, I'll use <xref
        ref="proposition-injective-kernel"/>. The identity element of
        <m>\RR^*</m> is 1. What matrices are sent to this identity?
        Well, to be sent to 1, the upper right corner of the matrix
        must be 1, which means that only the identity matrix is sent
        to 1. This means the kernel is only the identity in <m>G</m>,
        proving the function is injective. Since it is both injective
        and surjective, the function is a isomorphism. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Let <m>G</m> be the group of real-coefficient polynomial of
        degree at most 4. Define a function <m>\phi: G \rightarrow
        \RR^5</m> by the following equation (where I am writing
        vectors in horizontal notation).
        <me>
          \phi(ax^4 + bx^3 + cx^2 + dx + e) = (a,b,c,d,e)
        </me>
        I claim that this is an isomorphism. First, let me prove the
        homomorphism condition (the operation is addition for both
        grous).
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \phi( p_1 + p_2) \amp = 
            \phi( (a_1x^4 + b_11^3 + c_1x^2 + d_1x + e_1)  + 
            (a_2x^4 + b_2x^3 + c_2x^2 + d_2x + e_2)) 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = \phi ( (a_1 + a_2)x^4 + (b_1 + b_2)x^3 + (c_1 +
            c_2)x^2 + (d_1 + d_2) x + (e_1 + e_2)) = (a_1 + a_2, b_1 +
            b_2, c_1 + c_2, d_1 + d_2, e_1 + e_2) 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp (a_1, b_1, c_1, d_1, e_1) + (a_2, b_2, c_2, d_2, e_2)
            = \phi(p_1) + \phi(p_2)
          </mrow>
        </md>
        The steps here are: doing the polynomial addition; applying
        the function, pulling apart the vector into the addition of
        two vectors with ordinary vector addition; and then realizing
        the two pieces are just the image of the function on the two
        polynomials. This prove the homomorphism condition.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now I need to prove bijectivity. For sujectivity, let
        <m>(a,b,c,d,e)</m> be any vector in <m>\RR^5</m>. Then <m>ax^4
        + bx^3 + cx^2 + dx + e</m> is a polynomial of degree at most
        4 and the function applied to this polynomial is precisely the
        vector <m>(a,b,c,d,e)</m>. This proves that any vector in
        <m>\RR^5</m> is in the image of the function, so the function
        is surjective. 
      </p>
      <p>
        For injectivity, again I'll use <xref
        ref="proposition-injective-kernel"/>. What is the kernel of
        this homomorphism? The identity element of <m>\RR^5</m> is the
        zero vector <m>(0,0,0,0,0)</m>. For a polynomial <m>p</m> to
        be sent the zero vector, all coefficient of the polynomial
        must also be zero. Only the zero polynomial has this property,
        so the kernel is only the zero polynomial, the identity of
        <m>G</m>. This prove that the function is injective. Since I
        have both injectivity and surjectivity, the function is an
        isomophism and the two groups are isomorphic.
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Consider the function <m>\phi:Q \rightarrow S_8</m> defined by
        this table.
        <me>
          \begin{array}{c|cccc}
            n \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp i \amp -i \amp j \amp -j \amp k \amp
            -k \\
            \phi(n) \amp \Id \amp (12)(34)(56)(78) \amp (1324)(5768)
            \amp (5867)(1423) \amp (1526)(3847) \amp (3748)(1625) \amp
            (1768)(3546) \amp (3645)(1867) 
          \end{array}
        </me>
        The quaterions are determined by the relations between
        <m>i</m>, <m>j</m> and <m>k</m>. From <xref
        ref="definition-quaternions"/>, let me restate those
        relations.
        <md>
          <mrow>
            i^2 = j^2 = k^2 = -1 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            ij = k \hspace{1cm} jk = i \hspace{1cm} ki = j 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            ji = -k \hspace{1cm} kj = -i \hspace{1cm} ik = -j 
          </mrow>
        </md>
        If you check how the images <m>\phi(i)</m>, <m>\phi(j)</m> and
        <m>\phi(k)</m> behave, you will recover exactly these
        relations. I'm being a bit informal here, but doing so does
        verify that this is a homomorphism. For bijective, this is a
        perfect matching between two sets of cardinality eight, so it
        is a bijection. Therefore, this is an isomorphims. In this
        way, the quaternions are also isomorphic to a subgroup of a
        permutation group. 
      </p>
    </example>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
    <title>A Major Theme</title>
    <p>
      We've been talking about structure all through these notes.
      Isomorphism now finally gives us the language to clarify the
      discussion of structure. Two groups are <sq>the same group</sq>
      if their is an isomorphism between them: they have the same
      subgroups, same Caley tables, and so on. Therefore, to
      understand groups, we really only need to understand groups up
      to isomorphism. That is, it we know all the groups of a
      particular type up to isomorphism, then any new instance of a
      group of that type might be novel (it might be a matrix group
      where no previous instances involved matrices), but it's not
      actually new. It will be isomorphism to an existing group, its
      structure will be known, even if the particular of its
      definition are new. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Abstract algebra (and many part of pure mathematics) are
      motivated by holistic classification project. We have the
      definition of a group. What are all the groups? Asked naively,
      this become basically an impossible question. I can always
      invent new setting and new ways to define groups. However, if I
      ask the question only up to isomorphism, then the question is
      actually answerable. So all of group theory can be thought of as
      one enormous project: what are all the groups, up to
      isomorphism? 
    </p>
    <p>
      The example, already mentioned a couple time, of the dihedral
      groups is instructive. The dihedral groups are symmetries of
      polygons. However, they can also been seen as permutation of the
      vertices of the polygons and, as such, are isomorphic to
      subgroups of the permutation groups. They arise in their own
      unique way, from a reasonable and important geometric problem
      about shapes. But, if I want to just study their group
      structure, I can use the mechanics of cycles and transpositions
      from permutation groups if I wish. The permutation groups
      provide a way to capture the structure of dihedral groups. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The dihedral/permutation example is archetypical. When a new
      kind of group arises, I will always ask: what is this isomorphic
      to? If it is isomorphic to something familiar, then I already
      understand its structure and I use the familiar tools to work
      with that structure. If the new group is not isomorphic to any
      existing group, then I can celebrate because I have found
      something actually new: something with really novel structure
      not capture by anything I already know. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Consider also the groups <m>\ZZ/8\ZZ</m>, <m>D_4</m>, <m>Q</m>
      and <m>(\ZZ/20\ZZ)^*</m>. None of these four groups have
      equivalent subgroup lattices. If two groups are isomorphic, then
      all of their structure must be identical up to re-naming the
      elements: same Caley table, same subgroup lattice, so on.
      Therefore, I know that all four of these groups are
      fundamentally different structures. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Form this point, I want to know how much classification up to
      isomorphism actually can be accomplished. Much of the rest of
      the group theory section of this course will be devoted to
      showing you what structure and isomorphism theorems we can
      access. Group theory remains an active area of mathematical
      research and mathematicians keep working on precisely these kind
      of classification projects, so our knowledge of what groups
      exist, up to isomophism, continue to expand. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
    <title>Products of Groups</title>
    <p>
      I want to spend the rest of this section talking about
      isomorphisms of abelian groups. But before I do that, I need a
      new construction. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>G</m> and <m>H</m> be groups. The (cartesian) product
          of groups, <m>G \times H</m> is defined as follows.
          <ul>
            <li>
              As a set, <m>G times <m>H</m> is the usual set cartesian
              product. 
            </li>
            <li>
              The binary operation is defined componentwise. 
              <me>
                (g_1, h_1) (g_2, h_2) = (g_1g_2,h_1h_2)
              </me>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The cartesian product of two groups is also a group. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          I need to check the group axioms. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              Since the binary operation is component-wise,
              associativity is inherited from the associativity of the
              two operation. I can move the brackets in each component
              as necesary to prove associativity.
            </li>
            <li>
              The element <m>(e_g, e_h)</m> is the identity. I can
              check:
              <me>
                (e_g, e_h) (g,h) = (e_g g, e_h h) = (g,h)
              </me>
              A similar calculation works with multiplication on the
              right as well. 
            </li>
            <li>
              The inverse of <m>(g,h)</m> is <m>(g^{-1},
              h^{-1})))</m>. I can check this with the component-wise
              product: multiplication by this proposed inverse, both
              on the right and the left, gives the identity. 
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      The set product from <xref ref="definition-set-product"> and the
      group product share the same notation, <m>G \times H</m>. This
      is conventional, but it risks some confusion. The set product
      doesn't define a binary operation: it just defined ordered pairs
      of elements of the set. The group product add the binary
      operation, and is only defined, of course, if both pieces are
      already groups. Even so, most of the time the notation works
      relatively well, since the group product, when thought as just a
      set, is still the ordered pair: the set product. When I need to
      distinguish between the two product, I will do what I've already
      done in this paragraph<mdash/>use the phrases <sq>set
      product</sq> and <sq>group product</sq> to make it clear which
      product I want to talk about. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The group product is a way to put two groups together to make a
      new group. This is, obviously, an important piece of
      understanding the structures of group. Some cartesian products
      are familiar: <m>\RR^2</m> is the product <m>\RR \times \RR</m>.
      This was known as sets before, but this also works as additive
      groups.
    </p>
    <p>
      As with the set product <xref ref="definition-set-product">, the
      group product can be iterated. <m>G \times H \times K</m>
      consists of triples of elements, one from each groups, with
      componentwise multiplication. <m>G^n</m> is the product of a
      group with itself <m>n</m> times. This makes <m>\RR^n</m> also a
      group product. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The cartesian product comes with four important homomorphisms. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>G</m> and <m>H</m> be groups.
          <ul>
            <li>
              The inclusion on the first component, <m>i_1</m>, is an
              injective homomorphism <m>i_1: G \rightarrow G \times
              H</m> defined as follows. 
              <me>
                i_1 (g) = (g, e_h)
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              The inclusion on the second component, <m>i_2</m>, is an
              injective homomorphism <m>i_2: H \rightarrow G \times
              H</m> defined as follows. 
              <me>
                i_1 (h) = (e_g, h)
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              The projection onto the first component, <m>\pi_1</m>, is 
              a surjective homomorphism <m>\pi_1: G \times H
              \rightarrow G </m> defined as follows. 
              <me>
                \pi (g, h) = g
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              The projection onto the second component, <m>\pi_2</m>, is 
              a surjective homomorphism <m>\pi_2: G \times H
              \rightarrow H </m> defined as follows. 
              <me>
                \pi (g, h) = H
              </me>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      I'll leave the claims in the previous definition (that these are
      homomorphisms, that they are injective or surjective) up to you
      to verify if you wish. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The cartesian product is a way to build a larger group out of
      two smaller groups, <m>G</m> and <m>H</m>. However, it is not th
      only way to do such a thing. There are other products, and other
      understanding of how to put two groups together. In this course,
      we'll stick to the cartesian product, but I wanted to give you a
      very brief idea of how more general constructions are treated in
      group theory. The next definition is the main idea. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>G</m> and <m>H</m> be groups. An
          <term>extension</term> of <m>H</m> by <m>G</m> is any group
          <m>K</m> such that three things are true. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              There exist an injective homomorphism:
              <me>
                i: G \rightarrow K
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              There exist a surjective homomorphism:
              <me>
                \pi K \rightarrow H
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              The kernel of <m>\pi</m> is equal to the image of
              <m>i</m> 
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      The cartesian product satisfies the condition. The inclusion of
      the first component is the injective homomorphism, and the
      projection onto the second component is the surjective
      homomorphism. The image of the inclusion if <m>\{(g, e_h) | g
      \in G</m>. This is precisely the same as the kernel of the
      second project, since it is all elements of the product where the
      second component is the identity of <m>H</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The reason we need this extra machinery of extension is that
      many more groups other than the cartesian product can play the
      extension role of <m>K</m>. Each of them is essentially a way to
      put the two components together. I won't prove it here, but you
      can prove, for finite groups, that the cardinality of <m>K</m>
      is always </m>|G||H|</m>. As a set, <m>K</m> is always bijective
      to the set product <m>G \times H</m>. As a group, though, it can
      have a number of different and interesting groups structures,
      only one of which is the group product. I'll give one example.
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        I'll explicitly defined two functions. (I'll leave it to you
        to verify that these are homomorphisms, that the first in
        injective, and that the second is surjective.) 
        <ul>
          <li>
            Define <m>i: ZZ/4\ZZ \rightarrow D_4</m> by explicitly
            defining <m>i(0) = \Id</m> and otherwise <m>i(n) = R_n</m>
            for <m>n \in \{1,2,3\}</m>. 
          </li>
          <li>
            Define <m>\pi: D_4 \rightarrow ZZ/2\ZZ \rightarrow D_4</m>
            by this table.
            <me>
              \begin{array}{c|cccccccc}
                g \amp \Id \amp R_1 \amp R_2 \amp R_3 
                \amp F_1 \amp F_2 \amp F_3 \amp F_4 \\
                \pi(g) \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 
                \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 
              \end{array}
            </me>
          </li>
          <li>
            The image of <m>i</m> is the subgroup <m>\{ \Id, R_1, R_2,
            R_3 \}</m>. By looking at the table for <m>\pi</m>, this
            is precisely the kernel of <m>\pi</m>, so the third
            extension condition is satisfied. 
          </li>
        </ul>
        <m>D_4</m> is, however, not isomorphic to <m>\ZZ/2ZZ \times
        \ZZ/4\ZZ</m>. One easy way to tell is that <m>D_4</m> is not
        abelian, and the product of two abelian groups will always be
        abelian, so <m>\ZZ/2\ZZ \times \ZZ/4\ZZ</m> is abelian.
        Isomorphic groups have the same group structure, so a
        non-abelian group can never be isomorphic to an abelian group.
      </p>
      <p>
        In conclusion, <m>D_4</m> is some kind of combination of
        <m>\ZZ/2\ZZZ</m> and <m>\ZZ/4\ZZ</m>, but a stranger and more
        complicated combination than the group product. It is an
        extension. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Extensions are also a great example of the central role that
      homomorphisms play in group theory. An extension is a way of
      gluing two groups toegether into a larger group. The most useful
      and general way of describing that construction turns out to be
      using homomorphisms: the extension is defined by its two
      homomorphisms, one of them injective and one of them surjective,
      along with the alignment of the image of one with the kernel of
      the other. There are equivalent ways to do this
      construction<mdash/>in fact, many group theory books will talk
      about the slightly more concrete idea of semi-direct products
      instead of the very abstract extensions. But I've chosen to do
      it this way because extensions are the way in which such
      constructions are actually talked about by working
      mathematicians, and to demonstrate how the language of
      homomorphisms makes it all possible. 
    </p>
    <p>
      As I said, this last definition and the last few paragraphs of
      discussion are more than we will cover in detail in this course.
      I'm not going to give any more examples of extensions and I'm
      not going to ask you to work with them in the assignments. But I
      hope you found this interesting. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
    <title>First Results about Isomorphisms</title>
    <p>
      Now I want to prove a couple results about isomorphismss. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Any group of a prime order <m>|G| = p</m> is isomorphic to
          <m>\ZZ/p\ZZ</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Let <m>g \in G</m> be any non-identity element. By <xref
          ref="corollary-order-divides"/>, the order of <m>g</m> must
          be a factor of <m>p</m>. The only factors are <m>p</m> and
          1, and order 1 only happens for the identity, so the order
          of <m>g</m> must be <m>p</m>. This means that <m>g</m> is a
          generator, and the group can be written as follows 
          <me>
            G = \{e, g, g^2, g^3, \ldots, g^{p-1} \}
          </me>
          If I write <m>e = g^0</m>, then I can use this to defined a
          function <m>\phi: G \rightarrow \ZZ/p\ZZ</m>. 
          <me>
            \phi(g^k) = k
          </me>
          First, this, is a homomorphism. 
          <me>
            \phi(g^k g^l) = kl = \phi(g^k) \phi(g^l
          </me>
          Second, the exponents in the group are <m>0</m> to
          <m>p-1</m>. The perfectly match up with the standard
          representations of the integers moduloa <m>p</m>, so the
          function is bijective. Therefore, the function is an
          isomorphism. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      This is already a pretty significant result. I know now all of
      the possible structures of groups of prime order. For each
      prime, there is only one possible group: the cyclic group of
      that order. All prime order groups are cyclic and, therefore,
      abelian. Non-cyclic and non-abelian groups can only occur for
      groups with composite order. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>i: G \rightarrow H</m> be an injective homomorphism
          of groups. Then <m>G</m> is isomorphis to its image (which
          is a subgroup of <m>H</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          There is not actually much to verify here. The function
          <m>i</m> is already a homomorphism and injective by
          definition. The image is precisely the outputs of the
          function, to resricting the codomain from <m>H</m> to the
          image makes it surjective. Therefore, the map <m>i</m> is a
          bijective homomorphism from <m>G</m> onto its image,
          therefore an isomorphism. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The composition of two isomorphsisms is also an
          isomorphisms. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          I know from <xref
          ref="proposition-composition-homomorphism"/> that the
          composition of homomorphisms is also a homomorphism. I know
          from set theory that the composition of bijections is still
          a bijection. That's all that needs to be said for the proof. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
  </subsection>
</section>

<comment>
  <subsection>
    <title>The Structure of Finitely-Generated Abelian Groups</title>
    <p>
      Now I want to put all this theory into practice and prove a
      major structural theorem. In this section, we can completely
      classify all finitely-generated abelian groups. We do this using
      isomorphisms to the most familiar abelian groups: the integers
      and the integers modulo n. I'll structure this section with two
      substantial lemmas followed by the main theorem. 
    </p>
    <lemma>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>n \in \NN</m> with <m> n \geq 2</m>. By <xref
          ref="theorem-fundamental-arithmetic">, I can write <m>n</m>
          as its unique prime decomposition. 
          <me>
            n = p_1^{m_1} p_2^{m_2}\ldots p_k^{m_k}
          </me>
          Then there exists an isomorphism of groups. 
          <me>
            \ZZ/n\ZZ \cong \left( \ZZ/p_1^{m_1}\ZZ \right) \times \left(
            \ZZ p_2^{m_2} \right) \ldots \left( \ZZ/p_k^{m_k}\ZZ
            \right) 
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          I need to define the isomorphism
          <me>
            \phi: \left( \ZZ/p_1^{m_1}\ZZ \right) \times \left(
            \ZZ p_2^{m_2} \right) \ldots \left( \ZZ/p_k^{m_k}\ZZ
            \right) \rightarrow \ZZ/n\ZZ
          </me>
          This is the definition. In the right side, the symbol
          <m>\hat{p_i}</m> will stand for the product of all primes
          <m>p_1^{m_1} p_2^{m_1} \ldots p_k^{m_k}</m> except
          <m>p_i^{m_k}</m>. Equivalently, <m>\hat{p_i} =
          \frac{n}{p_i^{m_i}}</m>. 
          <me>
            \phi(a_1, a_2, \ldots a_k) = \hat{p_1} a_1 + \hat{p_2} a_2
            + \ldots + \hat{p_k} a_k \mod n
          </me>
          Now I need to prove the properties. I'll start with
          homomorphism. I consider this calculation.
          <me>
            \phi(a_1 + b_1, a_2 + b_2, \ldots a_m + b_m) = \hat{p_1}
            (a_1 + b_1) + \hat{p_2} (a_2 + b_2) + \ldots \hat{{p_k}
            a_k + b_k = 
            = (\hat{p_1}a_1 + \hat{p_2}a_2 + \ldots + \hat{p_k}a_k) +
            (\hat{p_1}b_1 + \hat{p_2}b_2 + \ldots + \ha{p_k}b_k)
            = \phi (a_1, a_2, \ldots a_k) + \phi(b_1, b_2, \ldots b_k) 
          </me>
          This works, so <m>\phi</m> is a homomorphism. 
        </p>
        <p>
          Next, I need injectivity. Again, I use <xref
          ref="proposition-injective-kernel"/>. What is the kernel of
          this homomorphism? I need to consider all elements of the
          product group wuth that 
          <me>
            \phi(a_1, a_2, \ldots a_k) = \hat{p_1} a_1 + \hat{p_2} a_2
            + \ldots + \hat{p_k} a_k = 0 \mod n
          </me>
          Obviously, setting all <m>a_i = 0</m> works. But are there
          any other possibilites that also work? For the sake of
          contradiction, assume there is another combination that
          works, where at least one of the <m>a_i</m> is non-zero.
          Being equal to zero modulo <m>n</m> means that, as integers,
          the left side is a positive multiple of n. 
          <me>
            \phi(a_1, a_2, \ldots a_k) = \hat{p_1} a_1 + \hat{p_2} a_2
            + \ldots + \hat{p_k} a_k = k(p_1^{m_1} p_2^{m_2} \ldots
            p_k^{m_k}) 
          </me>
          Now one of the <m>a_i</m> is non-zero. I haven't ordered the
          primes at all, so without loss of generalization, I can let
          <m>a_1</m> be a non-zero element. Then I re-arrange the
          equation. 
          <me>
            \hat{p_1} a_1 = - \hat{p_2} a_2 - \ldots - \hat{p_k} a_k +
            k(p_1^{m_1} p_2^{m_2} \ldots p_k^{m_k}) 
          </me>
          Now think about the prime <m>p_1</m>. <m>p_1</m> does not divide
          the right side: <m>\hat{p_1}</m> explicitly excludes any
          power of <m>p_1</m>, and <m>a_1</m> is some number in
          <m>\{1, 2, \ldots, p_1-1</m>, a list which cannot include any
          multiples of <m>p_1</m>. But <m>p_1</m> does divide the
          right side: all of these terms include <m>\hat{p_k}</m> for
          <m>k \geq 2</m>; these all include powers of <m>p_1</m>. The
          final term explicitly includes powers of <m>p_1</m>. This is
          a contradiction: I can't have an equation of whole numbers
          where a prime divides the right but not the left. Therefore,
          by contradiction, I assume that <m>a_i = 0</m> for all
          <m>i</m> is the only solution, which means that the kernel
          is just the element <m>(0, 0, \ldots, 0</m>, which means
          that the function is injective. 
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally, surjective follows from the size of the sets. Both
          sets have cardinality <m>n</m>. This is by definition for
          <m>\ZZ/n\zZ</m>. For the product, the cardinality of the
          product is the product of the cardinalities, which is
          <m>p_1^{m_1} p_2^{m_2} \ldots p_k^{m_k} = n</m>. An
          injective function between two sets of the same size must be
          also surjective: everything is sent somewhere unique, so I
          get a perfect matching. This function is bijective. This
          concludes the proof. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </lemma>
    <p>
      This is the first lemma. It tells me how to deal with finite
      cyclic groups. The second lemma start to deal with more
      arbitrary groups. It guarantees that there is a particular way
      to write elements of a finitely generate abelian group. 
    </p>
    <lemma>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be a finitely generated abelian group. 
          Let <m>\{a_1, a_2, \ldots a_m\}</m> be a minimal set of
          generators. (Minimal means that if I remove an element, the
          remaining generator cannot generate all of <m>A</m>, and that
          <m>m</m> is the smallest size possible for a list of
          generators.) Let <m>a \in A</m>. Then there is a unique set
          of integer exponents <m>\{r_1, r_2, \ldots r_m\}</m> such
          that 
          <me>
            a = a_1^r_1 a_2^r_2 \ldots a_m{r_m}
          </me>
          and, for any <m>i</m> such that <m>a_i</m> has finite order
          <m>d_i</m>, <m>r_i \in {0, 1, \ldots d_i -1 \}</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          First, let me show that any element can be written in the
          desired form. The <m>a_i</m> being generators means that any
          group element is a finite product of the <m>a_i</m> and
          their inverses. However, this is an abelian group, so I can
          reorder any product as I wish. This lets me groups all the
          <m>a_1</m>s and their inverses together, and likewise for
          all the other <m>a_i</m>. Some product of <m>a_i</m> and its
          inverses can be described by an integer exponent
          <m>a_i^{r_i}</m>. I conclude that any element has the
          desired form. 
          <me>
            a = a_1^r_1 a_2^r_2 \ldots a_m{r_m}
          </me>
          I also know that the <m>r_i</m> satisfy the conditions
          for the finite order generators, since the list <m>r_i \in
          \{ 0, 1, 2, \ldots d_i - 1\}</m> cover all the possible
          power of <m>r_i</m>.
        <p>
          Now I need to argue that this way of writing a is
          <emph>unique<emph>: there is only one set of such exponents
          <m>\{r_1, r_2, \ldots r_m\}</m> for each element <m>a</m>.
          For the sake of contradiction, say there are two ways to
          write a group element. 
          <me>
            a = a_1^r_1 a_2^r_2 \ldots a_m{r_m} = 
            a_1^s_1 a_2^s_2 \ldots a_m{s_m}  
          </me>
          This is actually different, so at least for one index,
          <m>r_i \neq s_i</m>. (The <m>s_i</m> also satisfy the
          condition for the finite order generators.) Without loss of
          generalization (since I can order the generators as I wish),
          I will assume <m>r_1 \neq s_1</m>. But then I can
          manipulate this equation by leaving <m>a_1</m> on the left
          and taking all the other generators to the right. 
          <me>
            a_1^{r_1-s_1} = a_2^{s_2 - r_2} \ldots a_m{s_m - r_m} 
          </me>
          Again without loss of generality, since the two sets of
          exponents were labels <sq>r</sq> and <sq>s</sq> arbitrarily,
          I can assume <m>r_1 - s_1</m> is positive. So I have a
          positive exponent of <m>a_1</m> which can be expressed in
          terms of the other generators.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </lemma>
    <lemma>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be a finitely generated abelian group. Then
          there exists the following isomorphism of groups. 
          <me>
            A \cong \ZZ^d \times \ZZ/n_1\ZZ \times \ZZ/n_2\ZZ \times
            \ldots \times \ZZ/n_k\ZZ
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          <m>A</m> is finitely generated, so there exists a finite set
          of generated <m>\{a_1, a_2, \ldots a_m\}</m>. I can assume
          this list is minimal: if I can remove an elemenet and still
          generate <m>A</m>, I will do so. 
        </p>
        <p>
          Then any element of <m>A</m> can be written as follows.
          <me>
            a = a_1^r_1 a_2^r_2 \ldots a_m{r^m}
          </me>
          I need the assumption that <m>A</m> is abelian for this to
          be valid: I need to move all the product of <m>a_1</m> to
          the front of this product to gather them together, and
          likewise for the other generators. 
        </p>
        <p>
          Now I claim that this way of writing a is
          <emph>unique<emph>: there is only one set of exponents
          <m>\{r_1, r_2, \ldots r_m\}</m> for each element <m>a</m>. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </lemma>
  </subsection>
</comment>
